taliban forces say they've taken  complete control of the last afghan  province resisting their rule  battles had been raging for days in the  mountainous province of pansher which is  north of kabul  the taliban spokesman zabihullah  said they had been seeking a peaceful  resolution but faced continued defiance  the national resistance front has denied  that the area has been captured and says  it is still fighting  when our delegations went there they  received negative answers and they  couldn't resolve matters through  negotiations and dialogue so the islamic  emirate decided to send military forces  to get rid of this violent nest of  terrorism we want a peaceful country and  if anybody causes problems or causes  insecurity anywhere in afghanistan the  islamic emirate of afghanistan will make  a decision and not allow anyone to  congregate and act against our  government let's go to speak to our  correspondent charles stratford who's  live in kabul uh charles  so do we believe this to be a true  statement uh and if so how significant  is this victory over panshir  well certainly this is the first time  that we've heard an official statement  from the official taliban spokesperson  we've had numerous claims made by  commanders  and taliban sources over recent days  so yes we are taking this seriously  there was some fairly comprehensive  details given in that press conference  by um by the by the press spokesperson  we've heard absolutely nothing since  then from the  leadership of the nrf it was last night  that a statement was put onto facebook  by ahmad masood the leader of the nrf  saying that he was wanting to negotiate  with the taliban  an indication of just how much pressure  those anti-taliban forces were under um  you know this is hugely significant if  indeed it is the case there have been  reports unconfirmed reports of of minor  clashes ongoing but as as we know the  taliban have never had full control of  this area the soviets during the 1980s  never had full control either so if the  taliban have indeed achieved this then  it is a huge historical significance but  of course there are big fears about the  civilian population of that area around  150 to 200 000 people  um the taliban spokesperson was  saying that there had been according to  him no civilian casualties he said that  uh panshiris as he calls them should be  treated with as much respect the same  amount of respect as uh people from  anywhere in afghanistan bear in mind  that pancheeris are mainly ethnic tajik  and the ethnic tajik community  population of this country represents  about a quarter  of the population so um obviously the  taliban trying to reach out it seems to  to to  in a call of unity after what we  understand has been days of heavy  fighting  um he said that there'd been food  shortages also inside the valley um and  he said that food would be supplied  to the area to people in the valley he  said that  electricity and communication would  probably be turned back on as well  it was  very much it wasn't a victory speech  although there was some fairly heated  language used in it as well that  possibly the international community and  afghans indeed will be looking at and  trying to interpret when he said that  this was the last in his words nest of  terrorism  that uh had been dealt with in the  country whilst at the same time sort of  building on this ideas that we've heard  consistently and was mentioned again in  the press conference of the taliban  wants to  form a  inclusive government a government that  brings in all the voices ethnic  minorities religious minorities of this  country which of course is so vital if  it is going to get legitimacy here in  afghanistan and the wider world  many thanks for that charles stratford  their first live in kabul
 

